From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:07:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] target: translate for debug as the guest kernel sees an
 address

cpu_translate_for_debug() walks the page tables in the CPU's current
translation regime, and on several targets that regime's privilege decides
whether an address translates at all, even for a debug walk that checks no
access permissions:

- arm64: under TCR_ELx.E0PD1 the TTBR1 half faults at EL0 before any
  permission check (aa64_va_parameters);
- RISC-V: a U-mode walk refuses supervisor pages, and an S-mode walk without
  mstatus.SUM refuses user pages (get_physical_address);
- MIPS: user and supervisor modes take an address error outside their
  segments (kseg0/1/2/3, xkphys, xkseg);
- PowerPC: problem state can't read supervisor pages (hash keys, BookE
  UR/SR, radix EAA privilege), and a radix kernel whose AMR blocks user
  pages (Linux's KUAP) can't read them;
- LoongArch: a direct-map window applies only to the privilege levels its
  DMW enables (Linux enables PLV0 only), and PTE.PLV refuses PLV3.

A debugger or an analysis that reads guest memory while the CPU runs user
code has no way to ask for the kernel's view without changing the CPU's
privilege, which is unsafe from another thread.

Add SysemuCPUOps.translate_for_debug_kernel and cpu_translate_for_debug_kernel():
the same translation in the regime the CPU's supervisor uses, with the
current page tables, whatever the CPU's privilege is, and without the checks
that depend on privilege. It changes no CPU state. Implement it for:

- Arm: an EL0 regime's privileged counterpart (E10_0 -> E10_1, E20_0 ->
  E20_2, E30_0 -> E30_3_PAN; M-profile User -> Priv), same tables;
- RISC-V: S-mode with SUM (MMUIdx_S_SUM, two-stage when virtualized);
- MIPS: kernel mode (ERL keeps its own view);
- PowerPC Book3S (hash and radix) and BookE 2.06: supervisor state, with
  translation on for Book3S and the address space kept for BookE; then
  problem state, for user pages a protection key blocks the kernel from;
- LoongArch: PLV0 (not in direct-address mode).

x86 needs none: its debug walk checks no privilege.

On PowerPC, BookE 2.06 and radix took the privilege of a translation from
MSR[PR] rather than from the mmu_idx: take it from the mmu_idx, which has
the same value for every access the CPU makes (hreg_compute_hflags_value).

For x86, add x86_cpu_translate_for_debug_cr3(): the debug walk with a given
page-table root and the CPU's other paging controls, for a caller that
knows another table of the same address space (the kernel half of a
page-table isolation pair). x86_cpu_translate_for_debug() calls it with CR3.

